sympy has no built-in operator to multiply two formulas as if they were matrices, because sym_a/sym_b are just scalar expressions in free symbols (n, k) with no shape attached. What you wrote — a Sum over a dummy index i of A(n,i)*B(i,k) — is the correct, standard way to do this; you just need a reusable helper instead of hand-deriving it each time:

def matmul_formula(f, g, row, col, idx, upper):
    return sp.Sum(f.subs(col, idx) * g.subs(row, idx), (idx, 0, upper))

sym_b = matmul_formula(sym_a, sym_a, n, k, i, n)

This substitutes the column symbol of f and the row symbol of g with the summation index, matching the standard rule (AB)[n,k] = sum_i A[n,i]*B[i,k]. Using n as the upper limit works here because Pascal's triangle is lower-triangular (binomial(n,i)=0 for i>n); for a general, non-triangular formula-matrix you'd need an explicit symbolic size N as the upper bound instead of n.

Without seeing the third code block it's hard to pinpoint the exact bug, but partial failure of repeated Part boolean Cut operations is a very common OpenCascade/FreeCAD issue with a few usual causes:

1) coincident/coplanar faces exactly at the cut boundary make the boolean algorithm numerically unstable — give the dovetail cutting tool a tiny overlap (e.g. 0.01-0.05mm) into the female box instead of exactly touching faces;
2) reusing/mutating the same Shape object across loop iterations instead of a fresh copy (shape.copy()) can corrupt later cuts after earlier ones in the loop;
3) not refining the result — call result.removeSplitter() (or enable Boolean Refine) after each cut to clean up degenerate edges/faces that can break subsequent operations;
4) not validating — check shape.isValid()/shape.check() right after each Cut and log which specific box indices fail, since that tells you if it's geometry-position-dependent versus random OCC flakiness.

If you can post the third block, a more specific diagnosis is possible.
